{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2686 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d8f763a05cc761d0dde1d73db7a7992f1a7c2d93481587ee8f0edd355be85d28" :edb/db-before-t 2685 :edb/db-after-t 2686 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535998 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:35:40.729Z" 13194139535998 true] [17592186087808 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378813 13194139535998 true] [17592186087809 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378806 13194139535998 true] [17592186087809 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378827 13194139535998 true] [17592186087811 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378820 13194139535998 true] [17592186088168 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535998 true] [17592186088168 1100 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 2\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = 10e^{x}$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{x} + Bxe^{x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139535998 false] [17592186088168 1100 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=10{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139535998 true] [17592186088168 1145 "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x},$ we have that $\\lambda =1$ is the double root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10{e}^{x}.$  In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $1$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha ({x}^{2}+4x+2){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha ({x}^{2}+4x+2){e}^{x}-2(\\alpha ({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{x})+(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{x}) & =10{e}^{x} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x+2-2{x}^{2}-4x+{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{x} & =10{e}^{x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{x} & =10{e}^{x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =10 \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{x}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378806 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378807 13194139535998 true] [17592186378806 1089 "q-8099" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378806 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535998 true] [17592186378806 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=6{e}^{4x}.\n$$" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378806 1113 #uuid "1cd59461-d6db-57c3-84e4-ef10e9a068c9" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378806 1145 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =4$,  hence the complementary function ${y}_{c}$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{4x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $4$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (8+16x){e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (8+16x){e}^{4x}-2(\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x})-8\\alpha x{e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ (8+16x-2-8x-8x)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ 6\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ 6\\alpha  & =6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}+x{e}^{4x}.\n$$" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378807 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378808 13194139535998 true] [17592186378807 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378809 13194139535998 true] [17592186378807 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378810 13194139535998 true] [17592186378807 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378811 13194139535998 true] [17592186378807 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378812 13194139535998 true] [17592186378807 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535998 true] [17592186378807 1041 "selection" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378807 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378812 13194139535998 true] [17592186378807 1110 #uuid "4d1fa2e0-9c88-51a3-b933-c8c816cb081e" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378808 1034 "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}-2{e}^{4x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378808 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378808 1111 #uuid "ff7abd06-2b44-5056-9ab8-de0a3c016dba" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378809 1034 "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378809 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378809 1111 #uuid "d95ef9b8-e044-562a-b6d9-164991953aba" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378810 1034 "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}+2{e}^{4x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378810 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378810 1111 #uuid "25e59566-f703-5a9f-a0a6-83c58a53a3a3" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378811 1034 "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}+{e}^{4x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378811 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378811 1111 #uuid "55d818ac-b75b-5bbc-a975-8fbd0b864e65" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378812 1034 "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}+x{e}^{4x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378812 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378812 1111 #uuid "0ce8b116-01ef-5dd4-b47e-7ca5f852fdc0" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378813 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378814 13194139535998 true] [17592186378813 1089 "q-8101" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378813 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535998 true] [17592186378813 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=21{e}^{3x}.\n$$" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378813 1113 #uuid "236244f2-a048-542e-8782-505037690d4d" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378813 1145 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-4$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $21{e}^{3x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $3$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=3\\alpha {e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=9\\alpha {e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p}$, ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\alpha {e}^{3x}+7(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})+12(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =21{e}^{3x} \\\\ (9+21+12)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =21{e}^{3x} \\\\ 42\\alpha  & =21 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}.\n$$" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378814 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378815 13194139535998 true] [17592186378814 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378816 13194139535998 true] [17592186378814 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378817 13194139535998 true] [17592186378814 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378818 13194139535998 true] [17592186378814 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378819 13194139535998 true] [17592186378814 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535998 true] [17592186378814 1041 "selection" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378814 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378819 13194139535998 true] [17592186378814 1110 #uuid "01776914-c596-531d-bed4-0103bd0cec77" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378815 1034 "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{3x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378815 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378815 1111 #uuid "17b9366a-2d69-578d-8bd4-d6324254fc70" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378816 1034 "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}-{e}^{3x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378816 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378816 1111 #uuid "469c1f9f-1f1d-5a2a-8903-376cb5c18a26" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378817 1034 "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{4x}+{e}^{3x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378817 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378817 1111 #uuid "4ddf1218-be8d-53f5-84df-261301e41847" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378818 1034 "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{3x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378818 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378818 1111 #uuid "dc219b6e-ce84-554b-865e-a99507d76bc0" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378819 1034 "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378819 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378819 1111 #uuid "9a8a472d-9a80-5857-b34c-b5808da6e09f" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378820 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378821 13194139535998 true] [17592186378820 1089 "q-8109" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378820 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535998 true] [17592186378820 1100 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+8y=20{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=1,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378820 1113 #uuid "43bc963a-bacb-5dc3-8983-c6b7ccb15e63" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378820 1145 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $20{e}^{2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2,$ which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{2x}-4(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})+8(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =20{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha (4-8+8){e}^{2x} & =20{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =20{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha  & =20 \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+{e}^{2x}(-2P\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+10{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2P+2Q+10 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-4$ and $Q=0.$\n\nWe substitute this into the general particular integral and find that the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+0)+5{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378822 13194139535998 true] [17592186378821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378823 13194139535998 true] [17592186378821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378824 13194139535998 true] [17592186378821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378825 13194139535998 true] [17592186378821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378826 13194139535998 true] [17592186378821 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535998 true] [17592186378821 1041 "selection" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378821 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378822 13194139535998 true] [17592186378821 1110 #uuid "795e3a97-b6a5-56be-8bf8-e6fbd7b383d2" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378822 1034 "y=-4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5{e}^{2x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378822 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378822 1111 #uuid "46dbb446-ed7a-52a3-b6ed-c4bb78c91ee3" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378823 1034 "y={e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+5{e}^{2x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378823 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378823 1111 #uuid "39d846f5-99ea-5924-8d9b-bbaf8d873212" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378824 1034 "y={e}^{2x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{e}^{2x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378824 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378824 1111 #uuid "0a213fe1-847e-59b5-bac0-ffa149a06c9c" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378825 1034 "y=3{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{2x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378825 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378825 1111 #uuid "77697a4b-3bb7-571a-a5b1-54977a6a468a" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378826 1034 "y={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+5{e}^{2x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378826 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378826 1111 #uuid "7e243406-572c-569e-8035-55fd75af376f" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378827 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378828 13194139535998 true] [17592186378827 1089 "q-93677" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378827 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535998 true] [17592186378827 1100 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139535998 true] [17592186378827 1113 #uuid "94805db8-44e1-55e0-9567-e2835fac8614" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378827 1145 "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =2.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (4x+4){e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (4x+4){e}^{2x}-3(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})+2(\\alpha x{e}^{2x}) & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ (4x+4-3-6x+2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4x{e}^{2x}.\n$$" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378829 13194139535998 true] [17592186378828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378830 13194139535998 true] [17592186378828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378831 13194139535998 true] [17592186378828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378832 13194139535998 true] [17592186378828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378833 13194139535998 true] [17592186378828 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535998 true] [17592186378828 1041 "selection" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378828 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378829 13194139535998 true] [17592186378828 1110 #uuid "bfa94eec-f01a-5bc3-93b4-d3937b5d4691" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378829 1034 "{y}_{p}=4x{e}^{2x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378829 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535998 true] [17592186378829 1111 #uuid "d1bd506a-ea3b-599e-9a1e-9dd3460aba81" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378830 1034 "{y}_{p}=-3{e}^{2x}" 13194139535998 true] [17592186378830 110